# Clean Code Guidelines

## Naming & Clarity

- Use meaningful names that reveal purpose and usage
- Replace hard-coded values with named constants
- Avoid abbreviations unless universally understood
- Make code self-documenting rather than relying on comments

## Function Design

- Each function should do exactly one thing (Single Responsibility)
- Keep functions small and focused
- Use descriptive function names that explain the action
- If a function needs a comment to explain what it does, consider splitting it

## Code Organization

- Keep related code together in logical groups
- Organize code in a logical hierarchy
- Use consistent file and folder naming conventions
- Extract repeated code into reusable functions (DRY principle)
- Maintain single sources of truth

## Comments & Documentation

- Don't comment on what the code does - make the code clear
- Use comments to explain why something is done a certain way
- Document APIs, complex algorithms, and non-obvious side effects
- Keep comments up-to-date with code changes

## Error Handling

- Handle errors explicitly and appropriately
- Use meaningful error messages
- Implement proper validation and boundary checking
- Fail fast when encountering invalid states

## Code Structure

- Hide implementation details through proper encapsulation
- Expose clear, simple interfaces
- Move nested conditionals into well-named functions
- Use consistent indentation and formatting

## Quality Maintenance

- Refactor continuously to improve code quality
- Fix technical debt early before it compounds
- Leave code cleaner than you found it
- Write tests before fixing bugs to prevent regressions

## File Organization

- Keep files under 200-300 lines of code
- Refactor large files into smaller, focused modules
- Group related functionality together
- Use proper import/export organization
